From 7793f80abbb3f9f7d0ef097363747a324f339d2a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Samuel Price <thesamprice@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 15 Nov 2026 12:29:46 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 4/5] microblaze: add sibcall patterns

MicroBlaze had no sibcall patterns, so every call in tail position was
a brlid followed by the epilogue and an rtsd.  Add them.  The sibling
call epilogue is the normal one without the return: it restores r15,
the callee-saved registers and sp, then the call is a brid to the
callee, which returns through the restored r15 to this function's
caller.  Should the callee's address not be a symbol, the expanders
copy it to r12, a volatile register that no epilogue restores and no
argument uses, and the call is a brad r12.

microblaze_function_ok_for_sibcall rejects:
- calls without a decl, as the address may live in a register the
  epilogue restores;
- all PIC code, where the call goes through the PLT and the GOT
  pointer would have to survive the epilogue;
- interrupt_handler, fast_interrupt and break_handler functions, which
  return with rtid or rtbd, and save_volatiles functions, which must
  restore the volatile registers after the callee has run;
- calls to interrupt, fast interrupt and break handlers, which expect
  to be entered by an interrupt or by brki.

The epilogue now records its restores and its CFA changes in the CFI:
REG_CFA_RESTORE on each register load, REG_CFA_DEF_CFA when sp is set
from the frame pointer and when the frame is released.  Without these
notes, building newlib's ssp/gets_chk.c with -O2 -g failed in
maybe_record_trace_start: shrink-wrapping left one path to a tail call
without a frame, cross-jumping merged it into the sibling call
epilogue of a path with one, and dwarf2cfi saw two CFAs where they
joined.  The notes are on the normal epilogue too, not only the
sibling call one: cfgcleanup hoisted the r15 load common to both
epilogues of pr49390.c above the branch between them, and the hoisted
insn kept the notes of one, which failed the same way at -O3 -g.

The blockage that the separate fix for the dynamic area (setting sp
from the frame pointer only after the last access to it) emits in
microblaze_expand_epilogue is in the code both epilogues share, so the
sibling call epilogue gets it as well.

The strlen-pcmpbf tests scanned for brlid r15,strlen; strlen is in
tail position there and is now reached with brid.  Accept either.

Under QEMU at -O2, 1000 calls to a wrapper that tail-calls a leaf take
15012 instructions instead of 20012, and even/odd mutual recursion
1000 deep 3010 instead of 9011.  A wrapper that calls the leaf and then
tail-calls it takes 29010 instead of 28010: its argument must now
survive the first call in a callee-saved register, saved and restored,
rather than in its incoming argument slot, which a sibling call may
overwrite.  Over gcc.c-torture/execute and gcc.target/microblaze, 1646
files, text size at -Os goes from 3121940 to 3120312 bytes compared
with -fno-optimize-sibling-calls (85 files smaller by 1888 bytes, 18
larger by 260).

Tested on microblaze-elf and microblazeel-elf under QEMU:
gcc.c-torture/execute.exp and gcc.target/microblaze, with no new
failures, and microblaze.exp with four -mcpu variants on
microblaze-elf.  gcc.dg/sibcall-3.c, sibcall-4.c, sibcall-9.c and
sibcall-10.c, which check that a sibling call reuses the frame, now
pass.

Assisted-by: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>

